<body>

<div class="header">
  <h1>Amazon Bestselling Books: Price vs. Rating Trend Analysis</h1>
  <div class="subtitle">A Decade of Bestseller Data (2009–2019) — Pricing Strategy & Customer Satisfaction Insights</div>
  <div class="date">Report Generated: April 23, 2026 &nbsp;|&nbsp; Data Source: Amazon Top 50 Bestsellers (Annual)</div>
</div>

<div class="container">

  <!-- EXECUTIVE SUMMARY KPIs -->
  <div class="kpi-grid">
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-label">Total Entries</div>
      <div class="kpi-value">550</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">50 books × 11 years</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card accent2">
      <div class="kpi-label">Unique Titles</div>
      <div class="kpi-value">351</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">by 248 unique authors</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-label">Avg. Price</div>
      <div class="kpi-value">$13.10</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">Median: $11.00</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card accent2">
      <div class="kpi-label">Avg. User Rating</div>
      <div class="kpi-value">4.618</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">Range: 3.3 – 4.9</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card success">
      <div class="kpi-label">Price–Rating Correlation</div>
      <div class="kpi-value" style="color:#d63031">−0.133</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">Weak negative (Pearson)</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-label">Avg. Reviews</div>
      <div class="kpi-value">11,953</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">Median: 8,580</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- SECTION 1: MARKET OVERVIEW -->
  <div class="section" id="market-overview">
    <h2><span class="num">1</span> Bestseller Market Overview (2009–2019)</h2>
    <p class="section-desc">High-level summary of the dataset spanning 11 years of Amazon's annual Top 50 bestseller lists.</p>

    <div class="compare-grid">
      <div class="compare-card fiction">
        <h3>Fiction</h3>
        <div class="compare-stat"><span class="label">Total Entries</span><span class="val">240</span></div>
        <div class="compare-stat"><span class="label">Share of List</span><span class="val">43.6%</span></div>
        <div class="compare-stat"><span class="label">Avg. Price</span><span class="val">$10.85</span></div>
        <div class="compare-stat"><span class="label">Median Price</span><span class="val">$9.00</span></div>
        <div class="compare-stat"><span class="label">Avg. Rating</span><span class="val">4.648</span></div>
        <div class="compare-stat"><span class="label">Avg. Reviews</span><span class="val">15,684</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="compare-card nonfiction">
        <h3>Non-Fiction</h3>
        <div class="compare-stat"><span class="label">Total Entries</span><span class="val">310</span></div>
        <div class="compare-stat"><span class="label">Share of List</span><span class="val">56.4%</span></div>
        <div class="compare-stat"><span class="label">Avg. Price</span><span class="val">$14.84</span></div>
        <div class="compare-stat"><span class="label">Median Price</span><span class="val">$12.00</span></div>
        <div class="compare-stat"><span class="label">Avg. Rating</span><span class="val">4.595</span></div>
        <div class="compare-stat"><span class="label">Avg. Reviews</span><span class="val">9,065</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="insight-box">
      <strong>Key Takeaway:</strong> Non-Fiction dominates the bestseller list (56.4% of entries) and commands higher prices (+$3.99 on average), yet Fiction achieves higher ratings (+0.053) and substantially more reviews per title (+73% on average). This suggests Fiction buyers are more engaged and slightly more satisfied, while Non-Fiction publishers extract more per unit.
    </div>

    <div class="chart-container" style="max-width:600px">
      <canvas id="genreDistChart"></canvas>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- SECTION 2: PRICE-RATING CORRELATION -->
  <div class="section" id="price-rating-correlation">
    <h2><span class="num">2</span> Price vs. User Rating: Correlation Analysis</h2>
    <p class="section-desc">Quantifying whether higher prices translate to higher customer satisfaction on the Amazon bestseller list.</p>

    <div style="text-align:center; margin: 20px 0 28px;">
      <p style="font-size: 1rem; margin-bottom: 12px;">Pearson Correlation Coefficient (Price × Rating)</p>
      <span class="corr-badge">r = −0.1331</span>
      <p style="margin-top: 12px; font-size: 0.88rem; color: var(--text-light);">
        By genre: Fiction <span class="corr-badge" style="font-size:0.9rem; padding:4px 12px;">r = −0.103</span>
        &nbsp; Non-Fiction <span class="corr-badge" style="font-size:0.9rem; padding:4px 12px;">r = −0.135</span>
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="chart-container" style="max-width:800px; height:420px;">
      <canvas id="scatterChart"></canvas>
    </div>

    <div class="insight-box">
      <strong>Finding:</strong> The relationship between price and rating is <strong>weakly negative</strong> (r = −0.133). Higher-priced bestsellers do not earn higher ratings — if anything, the cheapest titles ($0–$5) achieve the highest average rating (4.718) while the $16–$20 segment has the lowest (4.499). This pattern holds for both Fiction (r = −0.103) and Non-Fiction (r = −0.135), indicating that price is not a driver of customer satisfaction on the bestseller list.
    </div>

    <h3 style="font-size:1.1rem; margin: 24px 0 12px; color: var(--primary);">Average Rating by Price Segment</h3>
    <div class="chart-container" style="max-width:700px;">
      <canvas id="segmentChart"></canvas>
    </div>

